1
00:00:04,811 --> 00:00:05,790
Hi, I'm Elmor.

2
00:00:05,840 --> 00:00:11,375
This is a .NET 10 web API I built around
one idea: keep every feature small,

3
00:00:11,425 --> 00:00:13,359
separate, and easy to find.

4
00:00:13,409 --> 00:00:19,385
I'll show how it's built, how to run it
from scratch, and how AI tooling fits into

5
00:00:19,435 --> 00:00:20,435
the workflow.

6
00:00:22,595 --> 00:00:24,018
Here's the stack.

7
00:00:24,068 --> 00:00:26,183
.NET 10 and ASP.NET Core.

8
00:00:26,233 --> 00:00:29,388
FastEndpoints instead of controllers.

9
00:00:29,438 --> 00:00:31,987
FluentValidation for requests.

10
00:00:32,037 --> 00:00:35,885
ASP.NET Core Identity with JWT bearer
tokens.

11
00:00:35,935 --> 00:00:41,689
Entity Framework Core on SQL Server, plus
Dapper for one raw query.

12
00:00:41,739 --> 00:00:44,165
And Scalar for the API docs.

13
00:00:46,377 --> 00:00:48,895
The architecture is vertical slices.

14
00:00:48,945 --> 00:00:54,318
Instead of folders for controllers,
services, and repositories, each feature

15
00:00:54,368 --> 00:00:55,744
gets its own folder.

16
00:00:55,794 --> 00:00:59,169
Under Features there's Auth, Product, and
Users.

17
00:00:59,219 --> 00:01:03,785
Each slice holds its endpoint, request,
response, and validator.

18
00:01:06,002 --> 00:01:08,283
Here's the create product slice.

19
00:01:08,333 --> 00:01:12,653
In Configure, the endpoint declares
everything about itself.

20
00:01:12,703 --> 00:01:18,480
The route, the API version, the Admin
role, a limit of 20 requests a minute, and

21
00:01:18,530 --> 00:01:20,228
its OpenAPI description.

22
00:01:20,278 --> 00:01:25,886
HandleAsync maps the request, saves
through the DbContext, and returns a 201.

23
00:01:28,095 --> 00:01:30,290
Validation sits right next to it.

24
00:01:30,340 --> 00:01:35,868
A FluentValidation class says the title is
required, and can't pass 50 characters.

25
00:01:35,918 --> 00:01:39,387
If a request breaks a rule, the handler
never runs.

26
00:01:41,603 --> 00:01:44,787
Program.cs wires the shared pieces.

27
00:01:44,837 --> 00:01:51,069
Identity, the SQL Server context, JWT
validation, CORS policies, and

28
00:01:51,119 --> 00:01:52,362
FastEndpoints.

29
00:01:52,412 --> 00:01:58,645
Routes get an api prefix and a version
prefix, so you get paths like

30
00:01:58,695 --> 00:02:00,216
/api/v1/products.

31
00:02:00,266 --> 00:02:05,112
Two OpenAPI documents, split by version,
feed Scalar.

32
00:02:05,162 --> 00:02:09,412
And a custom serializer adds XML next to
JSON.

33
00:02:11,629 --> 00:02:15,527
The data layer handles audit and soft
delete in one place.

34
00:02:15,577 --> 00:02:21,176
On save, the DbContext stamps created and
updated dates with the current user's ID.

35
00:02:21,226 --> 00:02:26,758
And if you delete a soft-deletable entity,
it turns the delete into an update that

36
00:02:26,808 --> 00:02:27,915
marks it deleted.

37
00:02:27,965 --> 00:02:30,143
Endpoints never do this by hand.

38
00:02:32,346 --> 00:02:35,746
For one read, I used Dapper instead of EF
Core.

39
00:02:35,796 --> 00:02:41,766
The users list is a paged raw SQL query,
joined to roles, and mapped straight into

40
00:02:41,816 --> 00:02:42,816
DTOs.

41
00:02:44,397 --> 00:02:46,160
Now, setup from scratch.

42
00:02:46,210 --> 00:02:51,147
You need the .NET 10 SDK, pinned in
global.json, and a SQL Server.

43
00:02:51,197 --> 00:02:53,338
I'm using SQL Server LocalDB.

44
00:02:53,388 --> 00:02:54,622
The build passes.

45
00:02:54,672 --> 00:03:00,742
It does print a few warnings, including a
vulnerable transitive package, and I'll

46
00:03:00,792 --> 00:03:02,983
come back to that at the end.

47
00:03:05,189 --> 00:03:06,598
Next, configuration.

48
00:03:06,648 --> 00:03:10,101
The connection string lives in
appsettings.json.

49
00:03:10,151 --> 00:03:16,012
The JWT section is called ApiSettings,
with a secret, an issuer, and an audience.

50
00:03:16,062 --> 00:03:18,420
I've masked the secret on screen.

51
00:03:18,470 --> 00:03:24,403
The development file turns on the database
initializer, with Migrate and Seed both

52
00:03:24,453 --> 00:03:25,403
set to true.

53
00:03:25,453 --> 00:03:28,153
In production you'd keep Migrate off.

54
00:03:30,244 --> 00:03:35,641
For this demo I point the app at a fresh
database with an environment variable.

55
00:03:35,691 --> 00:03:36,814
Then, dotnet run.

56
00:03:36,864 --> 00:03:41,985
On startup, the initializer finds 5
pending migrations, applies them to the

57
00:03:42,035 --> 00:03:45,778
empty database, and seeds the Admin and
Customer roles.

58
00:03:45,828 --> 00:03:50,172
FastEndpoints registers 11 endpoints, and
the app is listening.

59
00:03:54,089 --> 00:03:55,276
Now the live API.

60
00:03:55,326 --> 00:03:59,134
This is Scalar, running against the app on
localhost.

61
00:03:59,184 --> 00:04:03,065
Version 1 has the auth, products, and
users endpoints.

62
00:04:03,115 --> 00:04:06,486
Switch to version 2, and the document is
empty.

63
00:04:06,536 --> 00:04:11,049
The wiring is there, but there are no
version 2 endpoints yet.

64
00:04:19,569 --> 00:04:21,316
First, register an admin.

65
00:04:21,366 --> 00:04:25,462
The request is plain JSON, and the
response says success.

66
00:04:28,639 --> 00:04:30,410
Bad input gets rejected.

67
00:04:30,460 --> 00:04:36,404
A bad email and a short password return a
400, with one message for each broken

68
00:04:36,454 --> 00:04:37,454
rule.

69
00:04:44,958 --> 00:04:45,908
Now log in.

70
00:04:45,958 --> 00:04:50,269
The response includes a JWT, which I'm
blurring on screen.

71
00:04:50,319 --> 00:04:56,283
It carries the user's ID and the Admin
role, plus an issuer and an audience, and

72
00:04:56,333 --> 00:04:58,213
it expires in seven days.

73
00:05:07,848 --> 00:05:11,321
Without a token, creating a product
returns 401.

74
00:05:11,371 --> 00:05:15,137
I set the bearer token in Scalar, and send
it again.

75
00:05:15,187 --> 00:05:18,269
Now it's a 201, with the new product's ID.

76
00:05:21,425 --> 00:05:25,434
An empty title returns a 400, with the
validator's messages.

77
00:05:25,484 --> 00:05:28,596
The slice's rules run before the handler
does.

78
00:05:35,560 --> 00:05:37,675
Now, content negotiation.

79
00:05:37,725 --> 00:05:41,052
By default the list comes back as JSON.

80
00:05:41,102 --> 00:05:46,768
Change the Accept header to
application/xml, and the same endpoint

81
00:05:46,818 --> 00:05:47,807
returns XML.

82
00:05:47,857 --> 00:05:52,447
Ask for text/csv, and it returns 406, Not
Acceptable.

83
00:05:59,432 --> 00:06:02,091
Last call before deleting, the users list.

84
00:06:02,141 --> 00:06:07,237
That's the Dapper query, returning the
admin I registered, with the Admin role.

85
00:06:16,096 --> 00:06:18,051
Now delete the product.

86
00:06:18,101 --> 00:06:19,845
The API returns 204.

87
00:06:22,488 --> 00:06:23,908
But the row isn't gone.

88
00:06:23,958 --> 00:06:28,572
A quick query shows it's still in the
table, flagged as deleted, with who

89
00:06:28,622 --> 00:06:29,964
created it, and when.

90
00:06:32,178 --> 00:06:34,967
Now, how AI-assisted development fits in.

91
00:06:35,017 --> 00:06:38,152
The repo has a CLAUDE.md file for Claude
Code.

92
00:06:38,202 --> 00:06:43,621
It states the stack, the commands, the
architecture, and the non-obvious rules.

93
00:06:43,671 --> 00:06:48,310
For example, don't hard-delete, and don't
set audit fields by hand.

94
00:06:50,518 --> 00:06:55,242
It also tells the assistant to read the
docs folder before generating code.

95
00:06:55,292 --> 00:06:59,444
There are four short convention docs:
features, API configuration,

96
00:06:59,494 --> 00:07:01,672
authentication, and the data layer.

97
00:07:01,722 --> 00:07:06,446
So generated code starts from written
conventions that a reviewer can check

98
00:07:06,496 --> 00:07:07,496
against.

99
00:07:09,207 --> 00:07:11,771
The .claude folder holds the tooling.

100
00:07:11,821 --> 00:07:16,433
A docs-reference-updater agent keeps
CLAUDE.md's doc list current.

101
00:07:16,483 --> 00:07:20,177
A create-docs command scaffolds a new
convention doc.

102
00:07:20,227 --> 00:07:25,263
A commit command writes Conventional
Commit messages, and asks before it

103
00:07:25,313 --> 00:07:26,263
commits.

104
00:07:26,313 --> 00:07:31,561
And skills cover EF Core migrations,
database updates, and moving usings to

105
00:07:31,611 --> 00:07:32,561
global usings.

106
00:07:32,611 --> 00:07:35,295
Plus skill-creator, for building more.

107
00:07:37,064 --> 00:07:38,014
One more.

108
00:07:38,064 --> 00:07:42,746
A Stop hook in settings.json runs a Python
script after each response.

109
00:07:42,796 --> 00:07:48,272
It regenerates the project structure tree
in the README, so the docs don't drift.

110
00:07:50,082 --> 00:07:51,118
Two honest notes.

111
00:07:51,168 --> 00:07:55,720
The repo has no automated tests yet, and
AI tooling doesn't change that.

112
00:07:55,770 --> 00:07:57,637
That's the next thing I'd add.

113
00:07:57,687 --> 00:08:02,366
And the build flags a vulnerable
transitive package, which I still need to

114
00:08:02,416 --> 00:08:03,416
update.

115
00:08:05,078 --> 00:08:09,246
If you're hiring engineers who use AI
tools with discipline, written

116
00:08:09,296 --> 00:08:14,333
conventions, repeatable commands, and a
habit of checking the results, I'd like to

117
00:08:14,383 --> 00:08:15,333
talk.

118
00:08:15,383 --> 00:08:16,697
The code is on GitHub.

119
00:08:16,747 --> 00:08:17,988
Thanks for watching.

